        {
            "number": 8,
            "revision": "794c9474e39afa879ed5ee280199bd744d9b4c99",
            "parents": [
                "e548bbe08b8205a905a1cde90c2a4c955149bfe4"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/21/87121/8",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Willy Tu",
                "email": "wltu@google.com",
                "username": "wltu"
            },
            "createdOn": 1769921773,
            "author": {
                "name": "Willy Tu",
                "email": "wltu@google.com",
                "username": "wltu"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Are there cases where the cleanup is not done? What's better about this `shared_ptr` approach?\n\nFrom my perspective, `shared_ptr` tends to make code less readable since it's harder to tell when the destructor will be called."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Willy Tu",
                        "email": "wltu@google.com",
                        "username": "wltu"
                    },
                    "message": "I think everything is cleaned up properly right now. This change was made to make sure that we don't continue using the fd after we close it already.\n\n- https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/entity-manager/+/87122\n\nWe could technically also just pass in the reference of the fd and set it to -1 after we close it, however, that can be undefined behavior when it goes out of scope."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "> This change was made to make sure that we don't continue using the fd after we close it already.\n\n\nThe intention makes sense to me, but can it be done in a simpler way?\nI agree that some kind of cleanup is needed since for example `writeFRU` has a lot of `close(...)` calls and some code path could slip through without closing the file.\n\n\nWhat about something like this:\n\n```\nstd::ifstream file(\"/sys/.../eeprom\"); // or similar code\nread_fru(file);\nfile.close();\n```\n\nThat avoids the complexity of `shared_ptr` and keeps things simple.\n\nAnother way to solve it could be to make an enclosing scope\n```\n{\n  std::ifstream file(\"/sys/.../eeprom\"); // or similar code\n  read_fru(file);\n  file.close();\n}\n```\nwhich would prevent variables from being visible to the latter parts of a function.\n\nWhat's your take on these alternative methods to fix it?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Alexander",
                        "email": "alexander.hansen@9elements.com",
                        "username": "pointbazaar"
                    },
                    "message": "Can you put a `Tested` statement to know how you tested this change."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Willy Tu",
                        "email": "wltu@google.com",
                        "username": "wltu"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                }
            ],
